WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.857
A sample is a smaller group used to learn about a population

00:00:04.857 --> 00:00:10.057
First define who belongs to that population and how they can enter the sample

00:00:10.057 --> 00:00:16.000
These three groups contain ten members each; omitting a group changes whom our evidence can represent.

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:25.615
In a simple random sample of six, every subset of six population members has the same chance of selection

00:00:25.615 --> 00:00:34.367
Random selection does not guarantee a perfectly representative individual sample, but it gives a defensible probability model for sampling variation.

00:00:35.667 --> 00:00:39.155
Stratified sampling randomly samples within predefined groups

00:00:39.155 --> 00:00:45.135
Taking two from each of our equal-sized groups ensures all are represented

00:00:45.135 --> 00:00:55.600
When groups have different population sizes, analysis generally needs their population-size weights rather than blindly giving every sampled group equal influence.

00:00:56.900 --> 00:01:02.490
A large convenience sample can still systematically miss parts of the population

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:10.410
Surveying only one group gives no direct evidence about the others, however carefully we average the responses

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:16.000
Sample size reduces random variation more readily than it repairs selection bias.
